<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yao Xing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">366–416</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Wenhuan of Later Qin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yao Xing was the second ruler of the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, from 394 to 416. He defeated the remnants of the Former Qin and the Western Qin and ruled much of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, where he brought the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a> in 401 to direct the translation of Buddhist scriptures into Chinese.</p></div>
